    "proseHtml": "<p class=\"lead\">Ballina anchors the coastal side of the Northern Rivers, a growing centre with the region's airport, a solid industrial base and Byron Bay just up the road. Road Linx Transport delivers into Ballina and the surrounding Northern Rivers, running freight down from Brisbane and the Gold Coast on our own trucks.</p><p>With the Pacific Highway upgrade making the run smoother than it once was, getting freight into Ballina is more dependable than ever, and we make the most of it.</p><h2>Freight for Ballina and the Northern Rivers</h2><p>Ballina's economy mixes agriculture, tourism and trade. The region is macadamia country, there's a steady tourism trade feeding Ballina, Lennox Head and nearby Byron, and construction keeps materials moving to sites across the area. The Southern Cross industrial estate is a hub for local business, and the Ballina Byron Gateway Airport adds another layer of activity.</p><p>We handle the freight that keeps it all supplied, from palletised stock and trade materials to retail and hospitality deliveries, with trucks from utes up to semi-trailers depending on the load.</p><h2>Direct from Brisbane and the Gold Coast</h2><p>Ballina is a couple of hours south of the border, and we run it as part of our Northern NSW corridor. Freight comes down direct from Brisbane and the Gold Coast on one truck, so it isn't consolidated onto linehaul or passed between depots along the way. For Ballina businesses relying on southern Queensland suppliers, that direct link keeps timing steady.</p><h2>Smaller loads on a shared corridor run</h2><p>You don't need a full truck to get freight to Ballina efficiently. We can combine smaller consignments into a planned multi-drop down the corridor, so a few pallets to Ballina share an efficient run and still arrive direct rather than sitting in a depot.</p><h2>Tail-lift and timed delivery</h2><p>Ballina deliveries often go to shopfronts, cafes and fit-outs without a forklift, so our tail-lift trucks lower heavy freight to ground level safely. For retail and hospitality that can't take stock mid-trade, we run early morning and after-hours drops. The freight is restrained to rating on load-rated equipment, run under Chain of Responsibility, and closed off with a POD where you need it.</p><h2>Areas we cover around Ballina</h2><p>We service Ballina, West Ballina, Lennox Head, Alstonville, Wollongbar and out toward Byron Bay, plus the Southern Cross industrial estate. From Ballina we connect north to Tweed Heads, the Gold Coast and Brisbane, and inland to Lismore and across the Northern NSW region.</p><h2>Serving Byron and the coastal villages</h2><p>Ballina is the practical freight base for the coastal Northern Rivers, and that includes Byron Bay just up the road. Byron's tourism and hospitality trade pulls in a steady stream of stock, and the smaller villages between the two, like Lennox Head and Suffolk Park, need supplying too.</p><p>We deliver across that coastal pocket as one run, so a business supplying Ballina, Lennox and Byron isn't paying for three separate trips. It's the efficient way to service a string of towns that sit close together but each matter.</p><p>The Pacific Highway upgrade has taken a lot of the uncertainty out of the Ballina run. The smoother route means freight down from the border and Brisbane arrives on more dependable timing than it did a few years ago.</p><p>Ballina has its own regional airport in the Ballina Byron Gateway, and the town works as a distribution point for the coastal Northern Rivers because of its position and access. We use that same logic, treating Ballina as the hub we run the surrounding coastal deliveries from rather than servicing each town in isolation. It keeps the cost of covering the coastal pocket down and the timing tight for every stop on the run, from Ballina itself out to the smaller villages nearby.</p>",
