    "proseHtml": "<p class=\"lead\">Bundaberg is the northern anchor of the Wide Bay, a rich agricultural region famous for its sugar and rum and increasingly for its horticulture. It's a productive part of Queensland that both grows and manufactures, which makes for busy freight in both directions. Road Linx Transport delivers into Bundaberg and the surrounding district, running goods up from Brisbane and connecting the region south.</p><p>Bundaberg is our northern edge of the coastal corridor, and we service it as part of a network that reaches all the way back to Brisbane and the Port.</p><h2>Freight for a productive region</h2><p>Bundaberg's economy is built on the land. Beyond the well-known sugar and rum, the district is a major grower of small crops, macadamias and tree fruits, and that horticulture drives real freight demand for inputs, packaging and produce. There's also a solid industrial and trade base around Svensson Heights and the Port of Bundaberg at Burnett Heads.</p><p>We carry the freight that keeps it moving, from palletised stock and packaging to trade materials and general goods. Tautliners and semi-trailers handle the larger loads, while utes and tail-lift trucks manage local and site deliveries.</p><h2>Direct from Brisbane and the corridor</h2><p>Bundaberg is the furthest of our regular Wide Bay runs, and we carry freight up direct from Brisbane on our own trucks. It stays on one vehicle rather than being consolidated onto linehaul and passed between depots, which keeps timing dependable for a region a fair way north of the capital.</p><h2>Smaller loads on a shared run</h2><p>You don't need a full trailer to send freight to Bundaberg. We combine smaller consignments into a planned run up the coast, so your goods travel efficiently and still arrive direct.</p><h2>Tail-lift, hire and delivery around harvest</h2><p>Bundaberg deliveries range from farm sheds and packing houses to shopfronts, so our tail-lift trucks handle the drops without a forklift, and our semi-trailer hire gives local business extra capacity through harvest and busy periods without buying trucks. We also run early morning and after-hours deliveries where growing, packing or retail schedules call for it. Freight is restrained to rating on load-rated gear under Chain of Responsibility, and we hand back a POD when you need one.</p><h2>Areas we cover around Bundaberg</h2><p>We service Bundaberg and its surrounds, including Svensson Heights, Bundaberg North, Bargara, Burnett Heads and the surrounding growing districts. From here we run south to Hervey Bay, Maryborough, the Sunshine Coast and Brisbane.</p><h2>Freight that works around the harvest</h2><p>In a growing region, freight follows the seasons. Bundaberg's horticulture drives peaks where packaging and inputs have to be on hand before a harvest, and produce and goods move out heavily once it's on. Miss that timing and the whole run is thrown out.</p><p>We plan around those seasonal swings, getting inputs and packaging up from Brisbane ahead of the peak and carrying goods back south as they come off. For a district whose freight rises and falls with the growing calendar, working to that rhythm is what keeps supply dependable.</p><p>Bundaberg is the northern limit of our regular coastal runs, and distance is exactly why direct delivery counts. Keeping freight on one truck the whole way up avoids the delays that stack up when goods get passed between depots on a long haul.</p><p>Bundaberg has its own port at Burnett Heads, which adds a bulk and regional dimension to the district's freight on top of the road links south. It reinforces Bundaberg's role as a productive centre that both makes and moves goods, and we connect that local activity back down the corridor to Brisbane and the coast. For a district this far north, that dependable link to the capital is what keeps its supply chains from stretching thin over the long run up the coast.</p>",
