    "proseHtml": "<p class=\"lead\">Chinchilla sits deep in the Western Downs, known as the melon capital of Australia and, in more recent years, as a key town in the Surat Basin gas industry. That combination of established agriculture and energy-sector growth has made Chinchilla a busier freight destination than its size suggests. Road Linx Transport runs freight out to Chinchilla along the Warrego Highway, connecting it back to Toowoomba, Brisbane and the coast.</p><p>It's the western edge of our Darling Downs corridor, and we service it directly rather than leaving the far towns to fend for themselves.</p><h2>Freight for agriculture and energy</h2><p>Chinchilla's economy has two strong pillars. It's productive farming country, famous for melons but also growing grain and running cattle, and it's a base for the coal seam gas industry that has grown across the Surat Basin. Both generate freight, from agricultural inputs, packaging and produce to materials, equipment and supplies for the gas sector.</p><p>We carry the range with the right gear, from tautliners and semi-trailers for palletised and bulk loads to flat-tops and drop-decks for machinery and oversize freight. Tail-lift trucks handle the deliveries to farms and sites without a forklift, which is standard out this way.</p><h2>The long haul made reliable</h2><p>Chinchilla is a solid run west of Toowoomba, and distance is exactly why a direct carrier matters. We run freight up the range from Brisbane and out along the Warrego on the one truck, so it isn't handed between depots or held up along the way. For a town this far out, keeping goods on a single vehicle from pickup to delivery is what makes timing dependable.</p><h2>Combined runs to keep costs down</h2><p>Sending freight a long way doesn't have to mean paying for a whole truck. We combine smaller consignments into planned runs along the corridor, so freight to Chinchilla travels efficiently alongside other Western Downs deliveries and still arrives direct.</p><h2>Delivering to farms, sites and yards</h2><p>Chinchilla deliveries go to farms, gas-sector sites, yards and shopfronts, so our tail-lift trucks lower heavy freight to ground level where there's no forklift. Our semi-trailer hire gives local operations extra capacity when they need it, and we run early morning and after-hours deliveries to suit agricultural and industrial schedules. Every load is restrained to rating under Chain of Responsibility, and a POD closes off the delivery when you want one.</p><h2>Areas we cover around Chinchilla</h2><p>We service Chinchilla and the surrounding Western Downs and Surat Basin district. From Chinchilla we connect east along the Warrego Highway to Dalby, Oakey, Toowoomba, Brisbane and the Port.</p><h2>A base for the western energy fields</h2><p>Chinchilla has grown well beyond its melon-farming roots into a service base for the coal seam gas fields spread across the western Surat Basin. That has brought steady freight for site supplies, equipment and the camps and operations that support the industry.</p><p>We run that freight out from Brisbane and Toowoomba alongside the town's agricultural loads, reaching a destination that many carriers treat as too far out to bother with. For Chinchilla, being on our corridor rather than off the edge of it is what keeps supply reliable.</p><p>Reaching Chinchilla reliably is about commitment to the corridor. Because we run the Warrego regularly rather than occasionally, a town many carriers treat as too far out stays on a dependable schedule for both supplies in and goods out.</p><p>Chinchilla's melon growing is famous enough to have its own festival, and produce seasons bring their own freight peaks for packaging, inputs and outbound goods. We plan around those seasonal swings the same way we do for the coastal growing regions, so supply into and out of Chinchilla stays steady when the district gets busy.</p>",
