    "proseHtml": "<p class=\"lead\">Hervey Bay is the growth engine of the Fraser Coast, a coastal city that swells with visitors heading to K'gari and keeps growing as more people settle there. That mix of tourism and steady population growth means a constant flow of retail, hospitality and building freight. Road Linx Transport delivers across Hervey Bay, running goods up the Bruce Highway from Brisbane and the south.</p><p>For a city that lives on visitors and new residents, keeping shelves stocked and sites supplied is the whole game, and that's freight we handle well.</p><h2>Freight for a growing coastal city</h2><p>Hervey Bay's economy leans on tourism and retail, with a large hospitality trade around Urangan, the marina and the esplanade, plus the retail centres serving a growing population at Pialba and Eli Waters. Add ongoing residential construction and you get plenty of freight moving into the city.</p><p>We carry the range it needs, from palletised retail stock and hospitality supplies to building materials for the new estates. Utes handle quick local runs, semi-trailers take the full loads, and tail-lift trucks deliver wherever there's no forklift.</p><h2>Direct up the Bruce Highway</h2><p>Hervey Bay sits north of the Sunshine Coast, and we run the corridor up from Brisbane regularly. Freight comes direct on our own trucks, staying on one vehicle the whole way rather than being consolidated onto linehaul. For Hervey Bay retailers and builders sourcing from Brisbane, that keeps deliveries dependable.</p><h2>Shared runs for smaller loads</h2><p>A few pallets to Hervey Bay don't need a whole truck. We combine smaller consignments into a planned run up the coast, so your freight travels efficiently and still arrives direct rather than sitting in a depot.</p><h2>Tail-lift and after-hours for local trade</h2><p>Hervey Bay deliveries often land at shopfronts, cafes and fit-outs with no forklift, so our tail-lift trucks bring heavy freight to ground level safely. Because the city's retail and hospitality can't always take stock during trade, we run early morning and after-hours drops so businesses along the esplanade and through the shopping centres are restocked before they open. Every delivery runs on load-rated restraint under Chain of Responsibility, with a POD to close it off on request.</p><h2>Connected across the Fraser Coast</h2><p>Hervey Bay and Maryborough together make up the Fraser Coast, and we treat the two as one local network, running between them and out to the surrounding towns. From Hervey Bay we connect south to Maryborough, the Sunshine Coast and Brisbane, and north to Bundaberg.</p><h2>Areas we cover around Hervey Bay</h2><p>We service Hervey Bay including Pialba, Urangan, Scarness, Torquay, Eli Waters and Point Vernon, along with the surrounding Fraser Coast. From here we run south to Maryborough and Brisbane and north to Bundaberg.</p><h2>Supplying a visitor and lifestyle economy</h2><p>Hervey Bay's population swells with visitors heading to K'gari and grows steadily as more people retire and relocate there. Both trends lean on the same thing, a retail and services economy that has to stay stocked through the peaks.</p><p>We keep that supply steady, running stock up the coast so shops, cafes and services along the esplanade and through the growing suburbs don't run short when visitor numbers climb. Matching delivery to the rhythm of a tourism and lifestyle town is part of servicing it properly.</p><p>Visitor numbers to Hervey Bay swing with the seasons and the whale-watching calendar, and retail supply has to swing with them. We lift delivery frequency through the peaks so shops and venues aren't caught short when the town fills up.</p><p>An ageing and growing population has built a substantial health and aged-care sector in Hervey Bay, and that brings its own steady freight for equipment, consumables and fit-out. We service those deliveries alongside the retail and construction work, since a growing lifestyle city needs more than just shop stock moving through it. Tail-lift access matters here too, since a lot of these sites take heavy items with no forklift on hand.</p>",
