    "proseHtml": "<p class=\"lead\">Maryborough is one of Queensland's oldest regional cities and a genuine industrial town, with a manufacturing heritage that still runs today, from rail engineering to sugar. Road Linx Transport delivers into Maryborough and the wider Fraser Coast, running freight up the Bruce Highway from Brisbane and connecting the city with the coast and the south.</p><p>For a working industrial centre like Maryborough, dependable freight isn't a nicety, it's part of keeping production moving, and that's what we're set up for.</p><h2>Freight for an industrial city</h2><p>Maryborough's economy has real depth. The city is a manufacturing base, including rail engineering that has built trains for the Queensland network, alongside sugar processing, engineering and a solid trade sector. That industry generates freight in both directions, with materials and components coming in and finished goods going out.</p><p>We handle it with the right equipment for the job, from tautliners and semi-trailers for palletised and full loads to flat-tops and drop-decks for machinery, steel and oversize items. Utes and tail-lift trucks cover the smaller and site-level deliveries.</p><h2>Up the Bruce Highway from Brisbane</h2><p>Maryborough sits north of the Sunshine Coast on the Bruce Highway, and we run the corridor between Brisbane and the Fraser Coast regularly. Freight comes up direct on our own trucks, so it stays on one vehicle the whole way rather than being consolidated onto linehaul. For Maryborough manufacturers and suppliers dealing with Brisbane and the Port, that direct link keeps supply chains steady.</p><h2>Combining loads on the corridor</h2><p>You don't need a full trailer to move freight to Maryborough cost-effectively. Smaller consignments can join a planned run up the coast, so a few pallets to Maryborough share an efficient trip while still arriving direct.</p><h2>Factory-dock to shopfront delivery</h2><p>Maryborough deliveries range from factory docks to shopfronts, so our tail-lift trucks handle the drops without a forklift, and our semi-trailer hire gives local industry extra capacity through busy runs without the cost of owning trucks. We also run early morning and after-hours deliveries where production or retail schedules call for it. Loads leave restrained to rating under Chain of Responsibility, and we provide a signed POD on delivery when it's needed.</p><h2>Areas we cover around Maryborough</h2><p>We service Maryborough and its industrial areas, along with the surrounding Fraser Coast including Tinana, Granville and out toward Hervey Bay. From here we run south down the Bruce Highway to the Sunshine Coast, Brisbane and the Port, and connect north to Bundaberg.</p><h2>Keeping production lines supplied</h2><p>In a manufacturing town, inbound freight timing is part of the production schedule. Components and materials that arrive late can idle a line, and finished goods that miss a run can hold up a customer order down the chain.</p><p>We treat Maryborough's industrial freight with that in mind, running direct from Brisbane and the Port so inbound materials land when production needs them and finished goods head out on schedule. For a working factory town, a carrier that understands the clock matters as much as one that turns up.</p><p>Maryborough's industrial base means a lot of its freight is heavy, dense or awkward, from engineering components to machinery. We bring the flat-tops, drop-decks and restraint to move that kind of load properly, not just palletised stock.</p><p>The Bruce Highway ties Maryborough into the coastal freight spine, and the city's rail and engineering heritage still shapes what moves. Heavy, fabricated and project freight is part of the everyday picture here, so we bring the trailers and restraint to handle it rather than treating the town as a palletised-only run. That mix of light and heavy freight on the one carrier is exactly what a working industrial town needs from its transport partner, and it is what we set out to be for Maryborough.</p>",
